        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"stendhal-le-rouge-et-le-noir-78daff51c5dcf1c7\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"The utterance explicitly presents as true Julien’s purported present feeling of intense love: “Je sens que je vous aime.” Its dominant force is therefore truth-conditional avowal, regardless of likely exaggeration or insincerity. It does not itself undertake a commitment, and the narration depicts it as remembered literary courtship language rather than an affect-driven verbal discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The avowal is direct speech but also substantially modeled on phrases Julien recalls from La Nouvelle Héloïse. That literary revoicing complicates sincerity and originality, not the core T/P/E classification.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Julien directly addresses Amanda, although his language is narratively framed as recitation or adaptation of remembered phrases from La Nouvelle Héloïse.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Julien has only just met Amanda and becomes bold during a rapidly developing flirtation. The narrator immediately explains that he is recalling phrases from an incomplete volume of La Nouvelle Héloïse and has effectively been reciting it to her for ten minutes. Thus the declaration is a stylized, likely exaggerated courtship avowal whose sincerity is doubtful. His blush and emotional excitement concern delivery, while the textual emphasis on remembered phrasing weighs against independently reflexive E force. Nothing in the declaration itself commits him to a continuing relationship or obligation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Je sens que je vous aime de l’amour le plus violent.”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit first-person present construction, intensified by “Je sens,” strongly supports a truth-conditional presentation of a loving state.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Julien songeait à se rappeler les phrases d’un volume dépareillé de la Nouvelle Héloïse… Sa mémoire le servit bien; depuis dix minutes, il récitait la Nouvelle Héloïse à Mlle Amanda.”\",\"supports\":\"The declaration is framed as remembered and recited literary language, supporting a calculated or theatrical avowal rather than expressive/reflexive discharge; this framing does not remove its T force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Amanda’s astonished look changes Julien’s courage into boldness, though he blushes deeply while speaking.\",\"supports\":\"The utterance occurs as an escalated flirtatious move. The blush and bold delivery show affective intensity but do not independently establish E or a relational undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The surrounding exchange shows that Julien and Amanda have only just met and are arranging signs and possible future contact.\",\"supports\":\"The declaration functions as accelerated courtship and likely exaggeration; there is no established relationship or explicit commitment sufficient for P.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as affirmative present-tense direct love language in Chapter XXIV of Stendhal’s Le Rouge et le Noir.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the bibliographic location and grammatical form of the classified occurrence.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T/P/E adequately captures the core force as a direct but theatrically borrowed avowal. Literary imitation, probable insincerity, and courtship strategy are contextual qualifications rather than missing core-force dimensions.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"Passion and blushing are not treated as sufficient evidence for E; remembered literary recitation provides positive counterevidence.\"}"
        }
      ],
